TI  - Depression Detection Based on Social Media Data and Gated Attention Fusion Network
AU  - L. Liu
AU  - L. Cai
AU  - W. Yang
AU  - W. Mu
T2  - 2024 International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Digital Libraries (AIDL)
AB  - Depression, a severe mental illness, poses a significant threat to individuals' physical and mental health and is a leading cause of self-harm and suicide. Despite the availability of scientific and standardized diagnostic and treatment methods, many potential patients do not actively seek medical treatment. With the advancement of the Internet and social networks, these platforms have increasingly become an environment for users to share and express their emotions and opinions. Consequently, utilizing social media images and text data to identify depression has emerged as a prominent research focus. Nevertheless, existing studies have not fully explored the interaction between images and texts, leading to suboptimal results. To address these issues, this paper introduces a novel model for identifying depression using social media data, leveraging a gated attention fusion network. The developed model incorporates three modalities: user behavior, text, and images. Image features emphasize text fragments through cross-modal attention, while an adaptive gated logic unit mitigate the influence of redundant information. Moreover, contextual information and user behavior are considered to improve depression recognition, thereby improving the model's overall performance. Based on our experiments, the proposed model outperforms the existing state-of-the-art model, achieving an accuracy of 97.4% on the Chinese dataset and 96.7% on the English dataset. Finally, ablation experiments validate the effectiveness of the model.
