AU  - S. Sharma
AU  - J. P. Kumar
AU  - S. Lakhanpal
AU  - A. Albawi
T2  - 2024 International Conference on Communication, Computer Sciences and Engineering (IC3SE)
AB  - In the expanding domain of affective computing, the quest for real-time human emotion recognition and behavioral prediction has been significantly invigorated by advancements in deep learning architectures. This paper presents a comprehensive examination of novel deep learning frameworks that are at the forefront of accurately discerning a spectrum of human emotions and predicting subsequent behaviors in real time. These architectures harness the power of convolutional neural networks (CNNs), recurrent neural networks (RNNs), and groundbreaking attention mechanisms to process and analyze complex emotional cues from multimodal data sources, including facial expressions, vocal intonations, and physiological signals. Enhanced by adaptive learning rates and novel regularization techniques, the proposed models demonstrate remarkable proficiency in mitigating overfitting and adapting to dynamic emotional states, thereby offering improved robustness and reliability over traditional methods. Empirical evaluations, conducted on diverse datasets, reveal significant enhancements in recognition accuracy and prediction speed, paving the way for real-time applications in areas such as interactive gaming, mental health assessment, and autonomous vehicles. Moreover, the exploration of transfer learning and generative adversarial networks (GANs) within these architectures provides insights into efficient training methodologies and the generation of synthetic emotional data for improved model robustness. This work not only advances the theoretical understanding of complex emotional recognition in AI but also illustrates practical implementations that stride toward empathetic and intuitive human-machine interactions.
