TI  - A Multimodal Depression Diagnosis Model Inspired by Traditional Chinese Medicine’s Observation and Inquiry
AU  - H. Che
AU  - L. Xiao
AU  - E. Gu
AU  - X. Fan
AU  - S. Ni
T2  - 2025 19th International Conference on Complex Medical Engineering (CME)
AB  - The diagnosis of major depressive disorder (MDD) remains a significant challenge in the field of medicine. However, current diagnostic methods primarily rely on clinical diagnosis by physicians and scale scoring, which are subject to high levels of subjectivity, high costs, and high rates of misdiagnosis. To address these issues, this study draws inspiration from traditional Chinese medicine’s “observation, auscultation and olfaction, inquiry, and pulse” principles to propose a multimodal MDD diagnostic model combining ‘observation’ (facial data) with “inquiry” (PHQ-9 questionnaire data). In this study, facial data features were extracted using DeepFace to train the Facial expert; PHQ-9 questionnaire data were used to train the Scale expert; facial data features and questionnaire data features were fused to train the Concatenation expert; and the Facial expert, Scale expert, and Concatenation expert were combined to train the Meta expert. The training results of the four experts were then trained using the Cooperation of Experts (CoE) framework to ultimately construct the Fusion expert. The results showed that the Fusion expert model had a Log Loss value of 0.0529 and an MDD diagnostic accuracy rate of 100%, indicating that the Fusion expert model significantly outperformed traditional diagnostic methods. This study provides novel insights and methods for the precise diagnosis of depression, demonstrating that the integration of traditional Chinese medicine and multimodal data holds promise for improving the accuracy of MDD auxiliary diagnosis.
