AU  - I. Ullah
T2  - 2025 IEEE 6th International Conference on Computer, Big Data, Artificial Intelligence (ICCBD+AI)
AB  - The global burden of bipolar and schizophrenia spectrum disorders demands innovative diagnostic tools, as traditional methods of the Mini-Mental State Examination overlook critical emotional biomarkers. This study introduces a multimodal AI system that enhances MMSE screenings by integrating real-time emotion recognition across facial expressions, ocular micro-expressions, vocal prosody, textual sentiment, and body language. Trained on specialized datasets of FER-2013, DIU Eye, RAVDESS, GoEmotions, and BOLD, the system employs cross-attention fusion and a 4-bit quantified phi-3-mini LLM with QLoRA adapters for efficient clinical inference. Results demonstrate 89% sensitivity for early dementia detection, an F1 score of 0.86 for emotion recognition, and 92% accuracy in identifying cognitive gaps, consequently reducing assessment time through emotion-adaptive questioning. The results are also validated against neurologist scoring, i.e., κ=0.91, which bridges cognitive and affective diagnostics, offering a scalable solution for resource-constrained settings.
