AU  - D. S. Dhakar
T2  - 2025 1st IEEE Uttar Pradesh Section Women in Engineering International Conference on Electrical Electronics and Computer Engineering (UPWIECON)
AB  - In today's world, anxiety and depression have become ubiquitous, affecting large number of populations worldwide. Although a considerable amount of work has been done on automatic detection of these mental health problems individually but comorbid detection of anxiety in depressed individuals still remains underexplored. This study proposes a two-phase framework for the comorbid detection of anxiety in depressed individuals. In Phase 1, it uses lexical features, affective indicators derived from Empath lexicon, and sentence level embeddings generated by a pre-trained transformer model for diagnosing depression and in Phase 2, anxiety is diagnosed in subset of depressed individuals by employing a pseudo-labelling strategy grounded in emotion features and clinical comorbidity rates from STAR*D study. Accuracy and F1 score metrics were used for evaluating the models. Results from Phase 1 were considerably moderate with F1 score of 0.64 for the depression detection, while Phase 2 showed significantly stronger performance by achieving F1 score of 0.97 for anxiety detection using a voting-based ensemble classifier. The models of both phases are trained by using the DAIC-WOZ dataset and focus solely on transcript-based features to ensure its applicability in resource constrained environments. These findings clearly depict the effectiveness of emotion-focused features and percentile-based thresholding in detecting the comorbid anxiety in the subset of depressed individuals. Overall, from this study we can see that transcript-based models can offer scalable, low-cost and interpretable solutions for comorbid mental health screenings and it also lays the foundation for then future multimodal or multi-task learning approaches in comorbid mental health assessment.
