TI  - Multimodal Deep Learning Framework for Early Stress and Mental Health Detection in Students Using Facial, Speech, and Behavioral Data
AU  - D. R
AU  - D. K
T2  - 2026 7th International Conference on Mobile Computing and Sustainable Informatics (ICMCSI)
AB  - Student mental health issues such as stress, anxiety, and emotional fatigue have become critical concerns in the education sector, particularly after the rapid transition to digital learning environments. Early detection plays a vital role in preventing long-term psychological impacts and improving student well-being. This paper proposes a multimodal deep learning framework that integrates facial emotion analysis, speech-based stress assessment, and behavioural activity features to predict early stress conditions in students. The system uses a hybrid architecture combining Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) for facial image processing, Bidirectional LSTM models for speech signal analysis, and a DNN classifier for behavioural features such as sleep duration, academic workload, and activity levels. A simulated dataset of 1,500 multimodal student samples was used to evaluate the model. Experimental results show that the proposed multimodal fusion model achieves 93.4% accuracy, outperforming unimodal approaches by $\text{1 2} - \text{1 8 \%}$. The findings highlight the effectiveness of combining visual, audio, and behavioral cues for early stress detection and provide a practical solution that can be integrated into educational monitoring systems, mobile health apps, and university counselling platforms.
